when it comes to good movies there's a  lot that rides on the actors  performances some roles can be played  without a great actor at the wheel and  when an actor is at the height of his or  her game one unforgettable performance  might be enough to nudge an okay movie  up into an award-winning film in the  case of these films these actors  performances were so strong that they're  practically perfect bringing the fury  she may not be the title character but  make no mistake the star of Mad Max fury  road is definitely Charlize Theron  Imperator furiosa she's the heart and  soul of George Miller's gorgeous  post-apocalyptic Road war movie of  course it was Miller who gave their in  such stellar material to work with but  it's not hard to imagine the character  falling flat in the hands of a less  capable actress with Theron behind the  wheel furiosa is every bit her name a  woman of determined fury hell-bent on  freeing immortan joe's brides from lives  of torment Farren creates a true modern  icon embellishing her determination with  a quiet empathy that shines through in  some of the film's calmer moments as  furiosa finally achieves her ends and  takes on the big bat she grunts what's  become to be the defining line of the  film remember me we sure do and we'll  continue to do so for years to come  making depression hilarious Ryan Gosling  seems to have dedicated much of his  career on screen to defying expectations  after blowing up thanks to a turn in one  of the most beloved romance films of all  time  Gosling took on a litany of strange  uncomfortable roles in order to distance  himself from the traditional career of a  leading man in Hollywood this has given  us films like the nice guys which may  well mark goslings finest hour as an  actor Gosling takes on the role of  Holland March a depressed alcoholic  private investigator in Los Angeles  circa 1977 March is vulnerable cowardly  and apt to self-harm there is a shocking  degree of drama sprinkled throughout the  performance considering the film as an  action comedy and Gosling sells marches  darkest moments just as expertly as his  funnier ones at the same time he's also  hysterical in the movie his may just be  one of the great comedic performances of  the decade  the kind of timing to perfectly foil  Russell Crowe's straight band routine  Munich one guy without his balls  it's a difficult contrast of balance  playing a life hating alcoholic who's  funny without compromising his  characters identifiability  it takes an actor as talented as Gosling  to pull it off burning it down even the  best films in the Marvel Cinematic  Universe are often marred by bland  unmemorable villains with vaguely  defined goals and personalities there  are occasional exceptions like Loki  surprise but for the most part Marvel  villains are uninteresting that changed  though when Michael B Jordan hit screens  is Eric kil mandra Stevens the  antagonist of Black Panther Jordan melds  swagger with terror painting kill monger  as a sympathetic character who's just a  few adjustments away from being the  film's protagonist his background as a  poor kid in Oakland California feels all  too familiar so much so that the  audience is more likely to relate to him  than to two challahs royal upbringing  his ultimate inability to distinguish  justice from aimless violence is all the  more heartbreaking because of how close  he is to being someone we love  Jordan's charming recklessness and  seething hatred make for a character who  feels doomed from the moment we meet him  yet seeing him finally meet his tragic  end still hurts  Jordans had some sizable performances  before of course but this one is  absolutely unforgettable besting the  braggadocio it's always incredible when  a young actor manages to not only hold  their own in a film featuring a slew of  major movie stars but manages to come  out as the standout performer in the  film there are a few better examples of  this than Hailee Steinfeld's turn in the  Coen Brothers remake of True Grit  Steinfeld scored the role of Mattie Ross  from a pool of 15,000 actresses and she  was just 13 when she began filming on  what was her first major movie role True  Grit tells the story of a young girl who  hires a drunk US Marshal named Rooster  Cogburn to help her track down the man  who killed her father  and while Steinfeld acts alongside heavy  hitters like Jeff Bridges Josh Brolin  and Matt Damon in the movie she steals  every scene she's in her take on Ross is  snappy  angry and fierce with Steinfeld  channeling the characters laser-like  focus on vengeance with the kind of  poise most actors four times her age  aren't capable of portraying some stars  go their entire lives without turning in  a performance as authentic as the one  Steinfeld crafted and she did it on her  feature film debut it doesn't get much  more impressive than that humanizing the  eight motion capture performances are  becoming just as impressive and  important as live-action ones and  there's no one better at putting on a  hi-tech computer suit than Andy Serkis  the actor has portrayed quite a few CGI  enhanced characters of notes including  Gollum from the Lord of the Rings  trilogy supreme leader snoke from the  new Star Wars trilogy and Caesar from  the Planet of the Apes trilogy and he  really shines in that last role as the  simian soldier not only is his  performance in the role among the best  of his career but it may just be the  most important motion capture acting of  all time throughout the films we see  Caesar evolved over the course of  several years in the beginning he's a  sweet loving primate whose intelligence  is obvious but whose otherwise pretty  innocent by the time we get to the last  film however he's soulful resident and  all-too-human Circus takes the character  on a journey from a young ape confused  by his place in the world to a grizzled  unbreakable leader of a new nation and  his performance never once falters along  the way yes  the special-effects crew does a stellar  job in transferring that performance to  animation but at its core circuses  tremendous work proves that he's a true  pioneer in motion capture acting  matching the tempo JK Simmons has pretty  much always been impressive from his  gritty work on the small screen to his  memorable foray into the superhero realm  to his versatile film appearances he  doesn't seem to miss even his insurance  company commercials are worth watching  but Simmons magnum opus is his role as  Terrence Fletcher in whiplash in the  film he portrays the jazz professor from  hell a maniacally focused and demanding  conductor who berates abuses to students  until they either achieve brilliance or  go insane his simultaneous mentorship  relentless antagonism of miles tellers  Andrew is both engrossing and repulsive  Simmons strikes the perfect balance of  mania never going so over-the-top as to  ruin the audience's suspension of  disbelief and yet there's a humanity  Simmons gives to Fletcher when he easily  could have been a cartoonish monster  there are plenty of great iconic movie  antagonists but Fletcher's realism makes  for one that will haunt any young  artists deepest insecurities elevating  the material it goes without saying but  Meryl Streep is one of the greatest  actors of all time  her filmography contains a multitude of  flawless performances which makes  picking just one sublime showcase next  to impossible that said there is one  character who simply would not have  worked without her and that's Miranda  Priestly in The Devil Wears Prada the  film is based on the novel of the same  name which tells the story of a college  graduate working as the personal  assistant to a tyrannical  editor-in-chief of a fashion magazine  the film and her role as Miranda  Priestly should have been a relatively  forgettable one but with Streep coming  in and bringing her a-game to the  picture the whole movie is lifted from  simple dramedy status to an absolute  must see that will stand the test of  time Streep's infinitely quotable Ice  Queen bears an unwavering facade of  steely perfection yet she lets that mask  slip just long enough to allow our young  heroine and the audience by extension  see the humanity that lies beneath all  that brass it's one thing to act well in  an already well crafted film but it  takes a truly magnificent performer to  turn a role like Priestley into an  oscar-nominated performance if Streep's  screen talents weren't already  documented enough her work in The Devil  Wears Prada proves that there's simply  no one as good or as effective as her  being a man there's very little to say  about pulp fiction that hasn't already  been set Quentin Tarantino's master  class in filmmaking is arguably the most  quotable movie of a generation and most  of the best lines came from samuel  l.jackson jules a righteous hitman who  finds himself in the midst of an  existential crisis over the course of  the film  Jackson is extremely prolific as an  actor and has appeared in a number of  Teno films but it's his turn here that  stands out as one of his finest hours as  an actor there's a calm controlled  intensity to jewels that lingers  throughout Jackson's performance he  calculates everything and almost always  has the upper hand which is why his  near-death experience in the film shakes  him to his core Jackson makes this  experience lesson unraveling and more of  a crossroads for the character it makes  his encounter with petty criminals Ringo  and Yolanda all the more riveting a  pivotal consider the controlled rage and  desperation that goes into every line  and what's Fonzie like mondo in a film  filled with memorable characters and  performances Jackson's stands out as the  definitive performance in one of the  great modern American films channeling  the insanity films based on Stephen King  books provide countless stellar villains  to choose from from Jack Nicholson's  Jack Torrance in the shining to penny  wise from it however there's one that  seems to stand out from the rest  even all these years later Kathy Bates  oscar-winning turn as Annie wilts in  misery Wilkes is fan culture gone toxic  well more toxic than it already is her  attachment to writer Paul Sheldon's  novels as fanatical leading her to  kidnap him after a car accident  oftentimes when a villain is so  memorable it's because the actor brings  a relatability one wouldn't expect to  the Roy the wilts is just not that kind  of woman her performance is of someone  so unhinged and unpredictable that it  frightens you to your core what do you  think I say when I go to the feed store  in town oh now Wally give me a bag of  the Effie pig feed and ten pounds that  [ __ ] leaked alcorn Bates plays Wilkes  is a woman who truly believes her  behavior is normal which makes it all  the more horrifying when she lashes out  at poor Paul Sheldon sure it's the leg  smashing seam that really got everyone  talking but it's Bates full package  villainy that rattled everyone's nerves  for the long haul  this is what you're looking for thanks  for watching click the looper icon to  subscribe to our YouTube channel plus  check out all this cool stuff we know  you love to  [Music]
 

